WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.958 --> 00:00:04.500
MODERATOR: What has
been your biggest

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:09.510
learning in creating all of
this Shiny for Python magic?

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:13.807
Oh man, the biggest
learning, there's been so much.

00:00:13.807 --> 00:00:15.390
A lot of different
components involved

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:16.230
in building this stuff.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:17.605
There's building
Shiny for Python

00:00:17.605 --> 00:00:20.040
itself, which was a really
good learning experience.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.800
I hadn't really done a whole lot
of Python programming before.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:27.270
But having the opportunity
to implement Shiny again

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.733
has been really interesting.

00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:30.150
It's sort of--
it's given us a lot

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.655
of chances to think about
hey, how do we-- do we really

00:00:32.655 --> 00:00:33.780
want to do things this way?

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:35.220
Can we do things a better way?

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:36.810
With this web stuff,
there's so much

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:41.220
JavaScript programming and
TypeScript for programming.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.190
And just learning what
the possibilities are here

00:00:44.190 --> 00:00:47.016
has been super
fun, and it's been,

00:00:47.016 --> 00:00:52.050
it's been really exciting to
push the boundaries of what's

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:52.690
possible.

00:00:52.690 --> 00:00:55.110
I feel like we're using stuff
right now that's sort of

00:00:55.110 --> 00:00:58.562
at the edge of what's
possible, like hey,

00:00:58.562 --> 00:01:01.020
I want to build something--
what I wish I had was something

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.090
that-- system that did this.

00:01:03.090 --> 00:01:05.880
And then being able
to go and build that,

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.720
that's been really fun.

00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.530
I feel like it's juggling
all the pieces, like in making

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:12.480
the transition from
exploration to Shiny,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.360
like knowing when to
stop exploring the data

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.920
and to move it into a Shiny app.

00:01:16.920 --> 00:01:20.730
And also, like just how to get
autocomplete for everything

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:21.880
has been really helpful.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.270
A lot of people think
about writing in VS code.

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.680
And you can't do it like in
a notebook in VS code, too,

00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:28.180
but I think the
reason notebooks are

00:01:28.180 --> 00:01:33.240
so useful is that oftentimes, I
want to autocomplete data, too.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.250
And that's not like
a static analysis.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.900
You can't just sit
down with a script

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:40.120
and autocomplete the
columns of your data.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.500
So to me, it's been that
dance of like auto-completing

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.070
the names of columns and stuff
and being able to build an app.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.680
That's a really weird,
actually I think uniquely,

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.690
data science space.

00:01:51.690 --> 00:01:54.540
It's hard to put
yourself in the shoes

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.210
of the users of
something like this

00:01:56.210 --> 00:02:00.450
if, by definition, you are
someone writing this app you

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:04.170
have experience and you're not
boots on the ground writing

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:05.440
these apps every day.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.680
And so it's very easy to
forget what's important

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.210
and to prioritize
the wrong things.

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:13.830
The biggest like
long-term learning thing

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:19.200
has been to not trust my
immediate intuition about what

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:22.590
is the most important thing
to prioritize in development

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:23.940
because I'm probably wrong.

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:27.390
As a data scientist,
I still think

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:30.630
that R is, you know, is
very much my tool of choice

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.430
if I'm doing that
kind of work, where

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:35.850
I'm working with a new
data set, trying to find

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.140
interesting things from it.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:40.680
But coming to Python
and now my day job

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.990
being more of a
software engineer,

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:48.090
where I'm the one
responsible for creating

00:02:48.090 --> 00:02:50.820
the software that data
scientists will then use,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.930
it's been a really nice
experience coming into Python

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.780
and there being
all of these kind

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:02.430
of really mature, sophisticated
libraries for building

00:03:02.430 --> 00:03:05.610
kind of the foundational
piece of what you would need

00:03:05.610 --> 00:03:07.140
for a framework like Shiny.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:10.260
And not only that,
but using VS code

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.600
with things like
GitHub Copilot and just

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:18.090
like the integrations with
navigating large Python code

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:22.230
bases in VS Code and all
the extension ecosystem,

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:24.330
provides a really
rich experience

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:29.720
for navigating through large
code bases and learning Python.

